# Lab 10 – Unsupervised Learning with K-Means and PCA

## Objective

The objective of this lab was to explore unsupervised learning techniques by applying **K-Means Clustering** and **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)**. These methods were used to identify patterns in unlabeled data and reduce the dimensionality of a dataset for easier visualization.

## What I Did

In this lab, I used the **K-Means** algorithm to group similar data points into clusters and applied the **Elbow Method** to determine an appropriate number of clusters. I then visualized the clustered data to better understand how the algorithm grouped observations. Next, I applied **Principal Component Analysis (PCA)** to the Iris dataset, reducing it from four features to two principal components while preserving most of the dataset's variance. Finally, I visualized the transformed data and interpreted the results.

## What I Learned

This lab introduced me to the fundamentals of **unsupervised learning**, where models discover patterns without labeled outcomes. I learned how K-Means clustering identifies natural groupings within data and how PCA simplifies complex datasets by reducing the number of features while retaining the most important information. These techniques are valuable for data exploration, visualization, and preprocessing before machine learning.

